%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: Watercolor spike -- Approach A: pure PostScript (issue #46)
%%BoundingBox: 0 0 620 620
%%EndComments

% Approach A prototype for the watercolor architecture spike (issue
% #46): real overlap-color-mixing using nothing but nested `clip`
% (a pure intersection operator, PLRM Level 1 -- no boolean-geometry
% library needed, since intersecting N clips *is* the AND of their N
% regions) plus #41's pkribbon for a hand-painted brush edge and a
% light noise-scatter paper grain. No renderer changes, no PostScript
% operator additions, no external tools -- and, unlike Approaches B/C,
% every mark here is verified against real Ghostscript (see
% WATERCOLOR.md's portability section).
%
%   pscat --page 620x620 docs/watercolor_prototypes/approach_a_pure_ps.ps \
%       --png docs/watercolor_prototypes/approach_a_pure_ps.png
%
% Ghostscript check (plain PS, no pscat-specific operators used;
% -dNOSAFER is needed only because this program `run`s two sibling
% library files by relative path -- gs's default SAFER sandbox blocks
% that with /invalidfileaccess, same as it would block any multi-file
% PostScript program, not something specific to this file):
%   gs -dNOPAUSE -dBATCH -dNOSAFER -sDEVICE=png16m \
%       -sOutputFile=/tmp/gs_check.png \
%       docs/watercolor_prototypes/approach_a_pure_ps.ps

(lib/artkit.ps) run
(lib/paintkit.ps) run
(docs/watercolor_prototypes/common_gesture.ps) run

1 1 1 setrgbcolor
0 0 620 620 rectfill

% --- paper grain, painted first so it shows in the gaps between washes
% (and *only* in the gaps -- an opaque wash necessarily hides whatever
% grain sits underneath it, which is the honest limit this technique
% hits: real watercolor paper shows through a translucent wash, this
% doesn't). Plain scatter, no noise2 field needed for isotropic grain.
7 srand
0.82 0.79 0.74 setrgbcolor
0 1 900 {
    pop
    newpath
    frnd 620 mul frnd 620 mul 1.0 frnd 0.5 mul add 0 360 arc
    closepath fill
} for

% --- base washes: three full, opaque circles. Painted in a fixed
% order (1, 2, 3) that would normally mean 2 and 3 occlude whatever
% they overlap -- that's the naive-layering problem the issue's "Why"
% names. The nested-clip pass below repaints every overlap region with
% its own blended color, so the final result doesn't depend on this
% order; it's here only so there's no gap while nothing has painted an
% overlap region yet.
Circ1 circpath Color1 setcolor fill
Circ2 circpath Color2 setcolor fill
Circ3 circpath Color3 setcolor fill

% arrA arrB colorArr paintpair -   repaint arrA's intersection with
% arrB using colorArr. `clip` intersects with whatever's already
% clipped, so two clips in a row is exactly "inside A and inside B" --
% no path-boolean library needed for this shape of problem.
/paintpair {
    /col exch def /arrB exch def /arrA exch def
    gsave
        arrA circpath clip
        arrB circpath clip
        col setcolor
        0 0 620 620 rectfill
    grestore
} def

% arrA arrB arrC colorArr painttriple -   same idea, three clips deep.
/painttriple {
    /col exch def /arrC exch def /arrB exch def /arrA exch def
    gsave
        arrA circpath clip
        arrB circpath clip
        arrC circpath clip
        col setcolor
        0 0 620 620 rectfill
    grestore
} def

Circ1 Circ2 Color12 paintpair
Circ1 Circ3 Color13 paintpair
Circ2 Circ3 Color23 paintpair
Circ1 Circ2 Circ3 Color123 painttriple

% --- brush-edge outlines (issue #41's pkribbon): trace each circle's
% own boundary as a closed centerline. pkribbon fills a closed
% subpath as two concentric loops (its own documented contract), so
% this reads as a wet ink ring rather than a flat vector circle.
3 srand
Circ1 circpath Edge1 setcolor << /Width 5 /Jitter 3 >> pkribbon
Circ2 circpath Edge2 setcolor << /Width 5 /Jitter 3 >> pkribbon
Circ3 circpath Edge3 setcolor << /Width 5 /Jitter 3 >> pkribbon

showpage
